From: mnemonic@eff.org (Mike Godwin)
Newsgroups: alt.bbs,comp.org.eff.talk
Subject:  Re: Logon disclaimers waiving the ECPA
Date: 10 May 1994 14:48:24 -0400
Message-ID: <2qokto$di8@eff.org>

In article <2qkhg1$m8i@edwin.bga.com>, David Smith <bladex@bga.com> wrote:

>A standard practice that I see on BBS systems is a logon disclaimer 
>saying that in no instances can email on the BBS be considered "private" 
>under the auspices of the ECPA.
>
>In effect, what these disclaimers are saying is that the provisions of 
>the ECPA do not apply to the systems.  
>
>The arguments I've seen are that this is similar to a contract that users 
>agree to abide before using the system and thus is legally binding.
>
>My question : am I really able to waive the ECPA simply by saying so?  
>How come?  And are there other civil statutes that I can nullify through 
>the use of disclaimers?  What are the guidelines here?

You're not able to waive ECPA at all. But your users are, and may do so if
they choose to continue using the system after you post this notice.

Note that this is a transcendently stupid move on the part of a sysop--it
eliminates his or her ability to claim ECPA protections if his or her
e-mail is wrongfully seized. And it may eliminate the ability of his users
to file a class action against the government.


--Mike



